/// \defgroup grp_w1_eac_huffman EAC Huffman codec
/// \brief EA Canada's Huffman codec: HUFF_encode and HUFF_decode with their
///        helpers, one of the codecs behind CompressionManager.
///
/// The codec belongs to EA Canada's CODEX library, which gives every codec
/// the same C entry points. huffcodex.h declares the Huffman ones: HUFF_is,
/// HUFF_size, HUFF_decode and HUFF_encode (it also declares HUFF_about,
/// which is not covered here). The encoder is huffencode.cpp and the
/// decoder huffdecode.cpp. \ref sub_wwlib_thirdparty describes the
/// libraries around them.
///
/// CompressionManager is the only user in the retail call graph, under
/// COMPRESSION_HUFF. CompressionManager::compressData writes its 8-byte
/// header (the tag "EAH" with a trailing NUL, then the uncompressed length
/// as a native int) and calls HUFF_encode with null options for the rest.
/// CompressionManager::decompressData recognises the tag through
/// CompressionManager::getCompressionType and passes the bytes after the
/// header to HUFF_decode. CompressionManager::getUncompressedSize reads the
/// length from its own header. HUFF_is and HUFF_size have no named caller
/// in BFME 2. Neither compressData nor decompressData has a named caller in
/// BFME 2 yet, so which BFME 2 data, if any, is stored Huffman-coded is not
/// established.
///
/// The code is Zero Hour's EAC source, which BFME 1 also carries. BFME 2
/// differs only in how it is built. BFME 2's encoder source calls malloc
/// and free directly, declared as CRT imports, where Zero Hour's source
/// writes galloc and gfree; in Zero Hour's gimex.h those are macros for
/// malloc and free, so the allocator is the same. huffdecode.cpp also
/// carries its own copy of the gimex.h helper ggetm.
///
/// \par Stream format
/// Bits are packed most significant first. A stream holds:
/// - a 16-bit signature. 0x30FB is a plain stream; 0x32FB and 0x34FB mean
///   the data was delta-coded once or twice before packing, and the decoder
///   undoes it. Bit 0x0100 (0x31FB, 0x33FB, 0x35FB) marks a composite
///   header with an extra length field first, which the decoder skips. Bit
///   0x8000 (0xB0FB to 0xB5FB) makes the length fields 32 bits wide instead
///   of 24.
/// - the uncompressed length, after the skipped field if there is one.
/// - the clue byte, the escape code of the stream.
/// - the code-length table: for each length from 1 bit up to the longest
///   code, a number giving how many codes have that length. The longest
///   length is not stored; the decoder stops once the counts form a
///   complete code.
/// - the symbol list, one number per code in canonical order (by length,
///   then byte value): how many byte values not yet listed lie between the
///   previous symbol and this one, counting round from 255 to 0.
/// - the data. Codes are canonical Huffman codes of at most 15 bits. The
///   clue's code is followed by a number: a nonzero count repeats the
///   previous output byte that many more times; zero followed by a 0 bit and
///   eight bits is a literal byte (used for bytes equal to the clue); zero
///   followed by a 1 bit ends the stream.
///
/// Numbers use a variable-length code: k zero bits, a 1 bit, then k+2 bits
/// added to a base of 4 times (2 to the k, minus 1). Numbers 0-3 take 3
/// bits, 4-11 take 5 bits, 12-27 take 7 bits, and so on. Encoder and
/// decoder agree on this only up to class 15; see HUFF_writenum.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Nothing here reads game data definitions; the format is fixed by these
///   functions. A tool that writes Huffman data for the game has to follow
///   the format above: one clue code, codes of 15 bits or fewer. The
///   decoder's code tables have room for 15-bit codes and no more.
/// - The decoder trusts its input and checks no bounds, so a damaged or
///   hand-made stream can crash the game instead of failing cleanly.
/// - The encoder has options for several run-length codes and for delta
///   codes, but HUFF_packfile always switches them off. HUFF_decompress
///   treats only the clue itself as special and would output those codes
///   as plain bytes, so enabling them produces streams the game misreads.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// - The codec is plain C: apart from the encoder's malloc and free it
///   makes no library or platform calls. Both directions read and write the
///   stream a byte at a time, so they do not depend on the host byte order.
///   The length in the CompressionManager header is a native int, which
///   does.
/// - The code assumes a 32-bit unsigned int for its bit buffers, and
///   HUFF_pack casts pointers to int; see HUFF_pack.
/// - HUFF_encode is never told the size of its output buffer and can write
///   more bytes than the input holds; see HUFF_encode.


/// \file Compression/EAC/huffcodex.h
/// \ingroup grp_w1_eac_huffman
/// \brief Public C interface of the Huffman codec: HUFF_about, HUFF_is,
///        HUFF_size, HUFF_decode and HUFF_encode.
///
/// Include codex.h first; the header stops the build otherwise. Compiled
/// as C++, the last argument of HUFF_decode and HUFF_encode defaults to
/// null. It also defines the qmin and qmax macros when they are not
/// already defined.


/// \file Compression/EAC/huffencode.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1_eac_huffman
/// \brief Huffman encoder: HUFF_encode and the file-local helpers that
///        analyse the data, build the code and write the stream.
///
/// Zero Hour's huffencode.cpp. BFME 2's source calls malloc and free
/// directly, declared as CRT imports, where Zero Hour's source writes
/// galloc and gfree; in Zero Hour's gimex.h those are macros for malloc and
/// free, so the allocator is the same. All encoder state lives in a
/// HuffEncodeContext that HUFF_encode allocates for one call; the file has
/// no global variables.
///
/// Call order: HUFF_encode, optionally HUFF_deltabytes, then HUFF_packfile,
/// which runs HUFF_init, HUFF_analysis (with HUFF_maketree, HUFF_treechase
/// and HUFF_minrep) and HUFF_pack (with HUFF_writecode, HUFF_writeexp,
/// HUFF_writenum and HUFF_writebits).


/// \file Compression/EAC/huffdecode.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1_eac_huffman
/// \brief Huffman decoder: HUFF_is, HUFF_size, HUFF_decode and the
///        file-local HUFF_decompress.
///
/// Zero Hour's huffdecode.cpp with one addition: a file-local copy of
/// ggetm, the helper HUFF_is and HUFF_size read the signature and length
/// fields with. It returns 1, 2, 3 or 4 bytes as a big-endian number, and 0
/// for any other count. The file defines its own copy so that the compiled
/// code matches retail, which keeps an out-of-line copy of the helper.
///
/// The file also declares a HuffDecodeContext structure that nothing uses;
/// HUFF_decompress keeps its state in local variables.


/// \class HUFFMemStruct
/// \ingroup grp_w1_eac_huffman
/// \brief A byte buffer and a length: how the Huffman encoder describes its
///        input and its output.
///
/// HUFF_encode builds two on its stack, one for the bytes to pack (the
/// caller's source, or a delta-coded copy of it) and one for the
/// destination. The input's length is the source size. The output's length
/// is reset to zero by HUFF_packfile and advanced by HUFF_writebits for
/// every byte written, so it ends as the encoded size, which HUFF_encode
/// returns. Neither buffer is owned by the structure, and nothing records
/// the output buffer's capacity.


/// \class HuffEncodeContext
/// \ingroup grp_w1_eac_huffman
/// \brief Working state of one Huffman encode: statistics, the code tables
///        and the bit writer.
///
/// HUFF_encode allocates one with malloc, passes it to every helper and
/// frees it before returning; nothing in it outlives the call. If the
/// allocation fails, HUFF_encode returns 0 without writing anything.
///
/// Fields, by role:
/// - Input: buffer and bufptr bound the bytes being packed; flen and ulen
///   hold their length. csum receives the sum of all bytes during analysis
///   but is never written to the stream.
/// - Statistics: count has three 256-entry parts. The first pass of
///   HUFF_analysis counts byte values, differences from the previous byte
///   and run lengths in them; the second pass counts how often each code
///   will actually be written, with the clue's entry counting runs.
/// - Huffman tree: tree_left and tree_right hold the children of the
///   internal nodes, which are numbered from 256 up so that a node number
///   below 256 is a byte value.
/// - Code tables, indexed by byte value: bitsarray is the code length (99
///   means the value has no code) and patternarray the bit pattern.
///   bitnum counts the codes of each length, sortptr lists the codes in
///   canonical order, codes is how many there are and mostbits the longest
///   length. HUFF_pack uses qleapcode as scratch marks while writing the
///   symbol list.
/// - Clue state: clue is the escape code and clues the number of
///   consecutive byte values used as escape and run codes (always 1 with
///   the options HUFF_packfile sets). dclue, dclues, mindelta and maxdelta
///   describe delta codes, which those options switch off. chainused
///   records whether code lengths had to be limited.
/// - Number coding: repbits and repbase, filled by HUFF_init, give the size
///   class and base of every number below 252.
/// - Bit writer: packbits and workpattern hold bits not yet written, masks
///   holds the low-bit masks for 0 to 16 bits, and plen counts bytes
///   written.


/// \fn bool HUFF_is(const void *compresseddata)
/// \brief Tells whether a buffer starts with a Huffman stream signature.
///
/// Reads the first two bytes as a big-endian number and accepts the twelve
/// signatures of the format: 0x30FB to 0x35FB and 0xB0FB to 0xB5FB. No
/// named BFME 2 function calls it. CompressionManager::getCompressionType
/// recognises Huffman data by CompressionManager's own "EAH" tag.
///
/// \param compresseddata Start of the candidate stream. At least two bytes
///                       must be readable; it is not checked for null.
/// \return true for one of the twelve signatures, false otherwise.
/// \see HUFF_size, HUFF_decode


/// \fn int HUFF_size(const void *compresseddata)
/// \brief Reads the uncompressed length from a Huffman stream header
///        without decoding.
///
/// The length field is 4 bytes when the signature has bit 0x8000 set and 3
/// bytes otherwise. For a composite header (bit 0x0100) the second length
/// field is returned, which is the one HUFF_decode uses. No named BFME 2
/// function calls it. CompressionManager::getUncompressedSize reads the
/// length from the CompressionManager header.
///
/// \param compresseddata Start of the stream at its signature. The
///                       signature is not validated.
/// \return The uncompressed length: the output buffer size HUFF_decode
///         needs.
/// \see HUFF_is, HUFF_decode


/// \fn int HUFF_decode(void *dest, const void *compresseddata, int *compressedsize)
/// \brief Expands one Huffman stream into a caller-supplied buffer.
///
/// The public decode entry point. It only forwards to HUFF_decompress,
/// which does the work. CompressionManager::decompressData calls it for
/// data tagged "EAH", passing the bytes after its 8-byte header; that is
/// the only caller in the retail call graph.
///
/// \param dest           Output buffer. It must hold at least HUFF_size
///                       bytes; nothing checks its size.
/// \param compresseddata Start of the stream at its signature. A null
///                       pointer decodes nothing and returns 0.
/// \param compressedsize Ignored: never read and never written. Unlike
///                       REF_decode, the number of input bytes consumed is
///                       not reported, and the input length that
///                       decompressData passes here does not limit
///                       decoding.
/// \return The uncompressed length from the stream header.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Decoding is limited by nothing but the stream itself, so only pass data
/// from trusted game files; see HUFF_decompress.
/// \see HUFF_decompress, HUFF_size, HUFF_encode,
///      CompressionManager::decompressData


/// \fn int HUFF_decompress(unsigned char *packbuf, unsigned char *unpackbuf)
/// \brief Decodes a complete Huffman stream: header, code tables, data and
///        the final delta undo.
///
/// The whole decoder, called only by HUFF_decode. \ref grp_w1_eac_huffman
/// describes the stream format.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - Header: reads the signature, skips the extra length of a composite
///   header, then reads the uncompressed length (24 or 32 bits), the clue
///   byte and the code-length counts, stopping when the counts form a
///   complete code. It then rebuilds the canonical code from the symbol
///   list.
/// - Tables: codes of up to 8 bits go into two 256-entry lookup tables
///   indexed by the next 8 input bits, giving the byte and its length. The
///   clue's entries carry a marker length that forces the slow path, as do
///   entries for longer codes. Longer codes, up to 15 bits, are found by
///   comparing the next 16 bits against one left-justified limit per code
///   length.
/// - Main loop: decodes short codes through the lookup tables in an
///   unrolled loop over a 32-bit bit buffer, refilled 16 bits at a time,
///   and falls back to the slow path for long codes and the clue. After
///   the clue it reads a number: a nonzero count repeats the previous
///   output byte, and zero is followed by a bit that selects a literal byte
///   or the end of the stream.
/// - Undo delta coding: for 0x32FB and 0xB2FB streams, and the composite
///   forms of them, each output byte is replaced by the running sum of the
///   bytes so far; for 0x34FB and 0xB4FB the running sum is applied twice.
///   This reverses HUFF_deltabytes.
///
/// \param packbuf   Start of the stream at its signature. Null returns 0.
/// \param unpackbuf Output buffer of at least the uncompressed length.
/// \return The uncompressed length from the header. The function does not
///         check that the data produced exactly that many bytes.
///
/// \warning No bounds are checked in either buffer. The output is written
///          until the end-of-stream code, whatever the header says, and the
///          bit reader fetches input ahead of use, so it can read a few
///          bytes past the end of a stream. A stream whose codes are longer
///          than 15 bits overruns the decoder's own tables.
/// \see HUFF_decode, HUFF_pack, HUFF_deltabytes


/// \fn int HUFF_encode(void *compresseddata, const void *source, int sourcesize, int *opts)
/// \brief Compresses a buffer with EA's Huffman coder.
///
/// CompressionManager::compressData calls it for COMPRESSION_HUFF after
/// writing its own 8-byte header with the "EAH" tag, and passes null
/// options. That is the only caller in the retail call graph, and
/// compressData has no known caller in BFME 2. The matching decoder is
/// HUFF_decode.
///
/// The encoder builds a Huffman code from the byte frequencies of the whole
/// input, so common byte values get short codes. Runs of a repeated byte
/// are coded as the clue code and a count when that is cheaper than
/// repeating the byte's code.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Allocates a HuffEncodeContext, delta-codes the input into a temporary
/// buffer when asked (HUFF_deltabytes, once or twice), and hands both
/// buffers to HUFF_packfile, which writes the whole stream. The temporary
/// buffer and the context are freed before returning.
///
/// \param compresseddata Output buffer. Nothing checks its size; see the
///                       warning.
/// \param source         Bytes to compress. Never modified.
/// \param sourcesize     Number of bytes in source.
/// \param opts           Optional. opts[0] selects delta coding: 0 for
///                       none, 1 for one pass, 2 for two passes. Null
///                       means 0.
/// \return Bytes written, including the stream header, or 0 when the
///         context cannot be allocated.
///
/// \warning Any other value of opts[0] packs the source unchanged but
///          writes a zero signature, which HUFF_is rejects. The temporary
///          delta buffer's allocation is not checked.
/// \warning The output can be larger than the input: the stream header,
///          the code tables and, when every byte value occurs, an escape
///          for each occurrence of the clue byte all add bits.
///          CompressionManager::getMaxCompressedSize allows only the input
///          length plus its 8-byte header, and compressData does not pass
///          the buffer size on.
/// \see HUFF_packfile, HUFF_decode, CompressionManager::compressData


/// \fn int HUFF_packfile(struct HuffEncodeContext *EC, struct HUFFMemStruct *infile, struct HUFFMemStruct *outfile, int ulen, int deltaed)
/// \brief Packs one buffer: sets up the encoder, analyses the data and
///        writes the stream header, then the packed data.
///
/// Called only by HUFF_encode. It initialises the bit writer and its masks,
/// calls HUFF_init, points the context at the input and runs HUFF_analysis
/// with a fixed option set: one clue code for runs and literals, delta
/// codes off, Huffman coding on, and a clue forced even when every byte
/// value occurs. Code lengths are limited to 15 bits. It then writes the
/// signature and length fields and calls HUFF_pack for the rest.
///
/// The signature follows deltaed (0x30FB, 0x32FB or 0x34FB). Lengths that
/// need more than 24 bits get the 32-bit forms (bit 0x8000). When ulen
/// differs from the input length, a composite header (bit 0x0100) records
/// ulen first and the input length second; HUFF_encode always passes the
/// input length, so it never writes one.
///
/// \param EC       Encoder context, owned by the caller.
/// \param infile   Input buffer and length.
/// \param outfile  Output buffer. Its length is reset to 0 and grows by one
///                 for every byte written.
/// \param ulen     Total length recorded in a composite header; see above.
/// \param deltaed  Number of delta passes applied to the input, 0 to 2. Any
///                 other value leaves the signature 0.
/// \return Bytes written, from outfile's length.
/// \see HUFF_encode, HUFF_analysis, HUFF_pack


/// \fn void HUFF_analysis(struct HuffEncodeContext *EC, unsigned int opt, unsigned int chainsaw)
/// \brief Chooses the clue byte and builds the final code: lengths,
///        canonical order and bit patterns.
///
/// Called once by HUFF_packfile before anything is written. Its result is
/// the code tables in the context, which HUFF_pack writes and uses.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - First pass: counts byte values, differences between neighbouring
///   bytes and the lengths of runs of a repeated byte (a run is cut at
///   30,000 repeats).
/// - Clue choice: the clue is the first value of the longest stretch of
///   byte values that never occur; the second-longest stretch is the
///   candidate for delta codes. When no value is unused and the force
///   option is set, the least frequent byte becomes the clue, and its real
///   occurrences are then escaped. The option bits then limit or disable
///   run and delta codes.
/// - First tree: HUFF_maketree builds a code from those counts. With more
///   than one run code, HUFF_minrep then drops run codes that would not
///   save bits.
/// - Second pass: counts what HUFF_pack will actually write. For each run
///   it compares repeating the byte's code with writing the clue and a
///   count, using the first tree's lengths, and counts the cheaper choice.
///   A second HUFF_maketree builds the final code from these counts.
/// - Length limit: while any code is longer than chainsaw bits, the
///   longest code below the limit and one of the longest codes both take
///   that shorter code's length plus one, and another of the longest codes
///   loses a bit. Each step keeps the code complete.
/// - Canonical code: if Huffman coding is disabled every code becomes 8
///   bits. The codes are then counted per length, sorted by length and byte
///   value, and given consecutive bit patterns, so that the decoder can
///   rebuild the code from the counts and the symbol order alone.
///
/// \param EC       Encoder context with the input set.
/// \param opt      Option bits. HUFF_packfile always passes the same
///                 fixed set, described there.
/// \param chainsaw Maximum code length in bits. HUFF_packfile passes 15,
///                 the most the decoder supports.
/// \see HUFF_maketree, HUFF_minrep, HUFF_pack


/// \fn void HUFF_pack(struct HuffEncodeContext *EC, struct HUFFMemStruct *dest, unsigned int opt)
/// \brief Writes everything after the signature and length: the clue byte,
///        the code tables, the coded data and the end-of-stream code.
///
/// Called by HUFF_packfile after HUFF_analysis has built the code.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - Code tables: the clue byte (8 bits), the number of codes of each
///   length from 1 bit to the longest, then the symbol list. Each symbol is
///   written as the number of byte values not yet listed that it skips past
///   the previous one, counting round from 255 to 0.
/// - Data: each byte is written with HUFF_writecode. A run of repeats of
///   the previous byte is written either as that many codes or as the clue
///   code and a count, whichever is cheaper. Runs are cut at 30,000
///   repeats, and the byte after a run is written normally.
/// - End: the clue code, the number 0 and the two bits 1 and 0, then up to
///   7 zero bits so that the last byte is complete.
///
/// The body keeps code that never runs: an older header layout that would
/// write the length, an options field and the clue ranges as numbers, and
/// branches for several run codes and for delta codes, which the fixed
/// options switch off. It also computes two values it never uses.
///
/// \param EC   Encoder context after HUFF_analysis.
/// \param dest Output buffer; the signature and lengths are already in it.
/// \param opt  The option bits; only the unused header layout reads them.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// One of the unused values is computed by casting the input pointers to
/// int, which a 64-bit compiler rejects or truncates. Dropping that
/// computation changes no output.
/// \see HUFF_analysis, HUFF_writecode, HUFF_writenum, HUFF_decompress


/// \fn void HUFF_init(struct HuffEncodeContext *EC)
/// \brief Fills the size-class tables for numbers below 252.
///
/// For each number below 252 it records its size class k (0 to 5; a
/// class-k number costs 2k+3 bits) and the base of that class: 0-3, 4-11,
/// 12-27, 28-59, 60-123 and 124-251. HUFF_writenum looks small numbers up here,
/// and HUFF_analysis, HUFF_minrep and HUFF_pack use the sizes to price runs.
/// HUFF_packfile calls it once per encode.
///
/// \param EC Encoder context.
/// \see HUFF_writenum


/// \fn void HUFF_maketree(struct HuffEncodeContext *EC)
/// \brief Builds a Huffman tree from the context's byte counts and stores
///        the resulting code lengths.
///
/// HUFF_analysis calls it twice: once for a first estimate and once for the
/// final code.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Every byte value with a nonzero count becomes a leaf, and the number of
/// them is stored as the code count. The two smallest counts are repeatedly
/// joined under a new internal node until one node remains, and
/// HUFF_treechase then turns depths into code lengths. The search for the
/// two smallest is a linear scan of the remaining list, so the work grows
/// with the square of the number of byte values in use. A single byte
/// value gets a 1-bit code. Values that do not occur get length 99, meaning
/// no code.
///
/// \param EC Encoder context with the counts filled in.
/// \see HUFF_treechase, HUFF_analysis


/// \fn void HUFF_treechase(struct HuffEncodeContext *EC, unsigned int node, unsigned int bits)
/// \brief Walks the Huffman tree and records each byte value's depth as its
///        code length.
///
/// Recursive: a node number below 256 is a byte value and receives the
/// depth; any other node passes depth plus one to both children.
/// HUFF_maketree calls it on the root with depth 0, or on the only leaf
/// with depth 1.
///
/// \param EC   Encoder context holding the tree.
/// \param node Node to visit.
/// \param bits Depth of that node.
/// \see HUFF_maketree


/// \fn int HUFF_minrep(struct HuffEncodeContext *EC, unsigned int remaining, unsigned int r)
/// \brief Estimates the fewest bits needed to write a run with the run
///        codes up to a given length.
///
/// When several run codes are in use, the code clue+k stands for k
/// repeats. This recursive search tries, for each such code from r down,
/// using it as often as fits or not at all, and prices whatever remains as
/// the clue followed by a count (20 bits for 252 or more). HUFF_analysis
/// uses the estimate to drop run codes that would not save bits.
///
/// It runs only when more than one run code is in use, which the options
/// HUFF_packfile sets never allow. HUFF_decompress treats only the clue
/// itself as special, so it would output such codes as plain bytes.
///
/// \param EC        Encoder context with code lengths from the first tree.
/// \param remaining Run length still to be written.
/// \param r         Longest run code that may be used.
/// \return The estimated cost in bits.
/// \see HUFF_analysis


/// \fn void HUFF_writenum(struct HuffEncodeContext *EC, struct HUFFMemStruct *dest, unsigned int num)
/// \brief Writes a number in the stream's variable-length number code.
///
/// The code is k zero bits and a 1 bit, then the number minus the base of
/// its class in k+2 bits; a number of class k costs 2k+3 bits. Numbers
/// below 252 take their class from the tables HUFF_init fills; larger ones
/// from a chain of comparisons up to class 18. Used for the code-length
/// counts, the symbol list, run counts and the 0 that introduces a literal
/// or the end of the stream.
///
/// \param EC   Encoder context.
/// \param dest Output buffer.
/// \param num  Number to write.
///
/// \note Numbers in class 16 and above (262,140 or more) do not survive a
///       round trip. For 16 or more leading zero bits the decoder's reader
///       takes a bit-by-bit path that counts one class too many, and the
///       encoder's bases for classes 17 and 18 are also 4 off the
///       4*(2^k-1) pattern. The encoder never writes numbers that large:
///       the largest are run counts, which stop at 30,000.
/// \see HUFF_init, HUFF_writebits


/// \fn void HUFF_writeexp(struct HuffEncodeContext *EC, struct HUFFMemStruct *dest, unsigned int code)
/// \brief Writes a byte as an explicit literal: the clue code, the number
///        0, a 0 bit and the 8-bit value.
///
/// Used by HUFF_writecode for bytes whose value is the clue itself, which
/// happens only when the clue had to be a byte value that occurs in the
/// data.
///
/// \param EC   Encoder context.
/// \param dest Output buffer.
/// \param code Byte value to write.
/// \see HUFF_writecode


/// \fn void HUFF_writecode(struct HuffEncodeContext *EC, struct HUFFMemStruct *dest, unsigned int code)
/// \brief Writes one byte value: its Huffman code, or an explicit literal
///        when the value is the clue.
///
/// \param EC   Encoder context.
/// \param dest Output buffer.
/// \param code Byte value to write.
/// \see HUFF_writeexp, HUFF_pack


/// \fn void HUFF_writebits(struct HuffEncodeContext *EC, struct HUFFMemStruct *dest, unsigned int bitpattern, unsigned int len)
/// \brief Appends the low bits of a value to the output, most significant
///        bit first.
///
/// The encoder's only output routine. Bits collect in the context, and
/// every complete byte is stored at the output's current length, which then
/// grows by one. Requests longer than 16 bits are split into the high part
/// and then the low 16 bits. Writing 7 zero bits flushes a partly filled
/// last byte, which is how HUFF_pack ends the stream.
///
/// \param EC         Encoder context holding the pending bits.
/// \param dest       Output buffer. Its capacity is not checked.
/// \param bitpattern Value whose low len bits are written.
/// \param len        Number of bits, up to 32.
/// \see HUFF_writenum, HUFF_writecode


/// \fn void HUFF_deltabytes(const void *source, void *dest, int len)
/// \brief Replaces each byte with its difference from the previous byte.
///
/// The first byte is kept as it is; arithmetic wraps modulo 256. It may
/// work in place, which HUFF_encode relies on for its second pass.
/// HUFF_decompress reverses it with a running sum, once or twice as the
/// signature says.
///
/// \param source Input bytes.
/// \param dest   Output bytes; may equal source.
/// \param len    Number of bytes.
/// \see HUFF_encode, HUFF_decompress
